Palmar tenderness and mild superficial blistering from unaccustomed manual tool use
Minor Ailments and Upsets
Palmar tense vesicles, Clear serous fluid, Unaccustomed mechanical friction history

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MAU-032
Reason for Visit: Acute localized hand pain and blistering requiring physical assessment to rule out palmar space infection or deep thermal burn.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted painful blistering on the palms impairing hand function. Urgent clinical assessment was necessary prior to the next in-hours period to rule out a deep palmar space abscess or infected hematoma, which could threaten digital viability if left unrecognized.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I was gardening all day and now I have these huge, painful water blisters on my palms.
History of Present Illness: Patient describes tense fluid-filled epidermal vesicles located over the palmar metacarpophalangeal joints evolving over the last 6 hours. Reports localized burning tenderness upon directly applied mechanical pressure. Describes a direct temporal association with repetitive friction from rigid tool handles during unaccustomed weekend work. Notes a rapid reduction in localized pain upon cessation of the mechanical activity. Complete absence of fevers, chills, or purulent drainage.
Functional Status: Fully independent, but unable to firmly grip objects due to pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: Temporary impairment in fine motor tasks.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 122/78 | HR: 74 | Temp: 36.6 C | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Alert, well-appearing, resting hands in an open, relaxed posture.
Physical Exam: Hand examination reveals tense fluid-filled epidermal vesicles containing clear serous fluid. Well-demarcated surrounding erythema is distinctly limited to the friction zone. The epidermal roof is entirely intact. Diagnostic triad confirmed: palmar tense vesicles, clear serous fluid, and unaccustomed mechanical friction history. Complete sparing of the dorsal hand surfaces. Normal passive and active digital range of motion. Capillary refill is brisk.
Environmental Observations: Hand tools visible outside the back door.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. No known allergies.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Palmar tenderness and mild superficial blistering from unaccustomed manual tool use (Friction Blisters) - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Deep Palmar Space Infection - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Superficial nature of the fluid, clear rather than purulent content, and lack of deep, throbbing pain or loss of normal palmar concavity.

Thermal Burn - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Clear history of mechanical friction rather than heat exposure, and localization strictly to weight-bearing/grip surfaces.

Herpetic Whitlow - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Localization to the palmar surface rather than the distal phalanx, and absence of clustered vesicles on an erythematous base.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The acute presentation of painful palmar vesicles heavily impaired the patient's functional status. An urgent after-hours evaluation was required to definitively exclude deep palmar space infections or compromised vascular status, which can masquerade as superficial lesions. Direct physical examination confirmed intact neurovascular supply and isolated the pathology to uninfected mechanical friction blisters, safely averting unnecessary surgical or emergency referral while providing appropriate barrier protection strategies.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Application of protective hydrocolloid blister dressings. Recommendation to leave the epidermal roofs intact to act as a sterile biological dressing. Use of oral NSAIDs for acute pain control.
Positive Behavioral Change: Wear appropriately fitted, padded work gloves before initiating manual labor, using the visual cue of placing the gloves directly on top of the tools to ensure compliance and prevent repetitive friction injuries.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): Hydrocolloid dressings.
Follow-up: Routine GP review if the fluid becomes cloudy or if red streaks develop.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to Emergency Department or call 000 if the hand becomes uniformly swollen, hot, and intensely painful, or if red streaks travel up the arm towards the armpit.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is a superficial mechanical injury; secondary infection remains a risk if the blister roof is avulsed.
Clinical Course: The fluid will be reabsorbed naturally over 3 to 5 days, and the underlying skin will harden and re-epithelialize within a week.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if the fluid inside the blister turns yellow or green, if foul odor develops, or if you develop a fever.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The friction caused the top layer of skin to separate, and your body filled the gap with fluid to cushion the raw tissue beneath. Popping the blister removes this natural bandage and invites bacteria in.
Actionable Steps: Do not pop the blisters or peel the dead skin. Keep the hydrocolloid dressings on until they fall off naturally. Avoid gripping tools until healed.

SMS INFORMATION

